    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ff3b0a0a-9488-56ab-9665-5f354dd960e4",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=3 x + 2 y$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$f_x(x,y)=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(3 x + 2 y)=3,\\qquad f_y(x,y)=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(3 x + 2 y)=2.$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field is $\\nabla f(x,y)=(3)\\,i+(2)\\,j.$\n\nNext, we sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2)$, $(2,2)$, and $(2,-2)$. Substitute their coordinates into $\\nabla f$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,2)&=(3)\\,i+(2)\\,j \\\\ \\nabla f(2,2)&=(3)\\,i+(2)\\,j \\\\ \\nabla f(2,-2)&=(3)\\,i+(2)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, draw the vectors starting at their corresponding points:\n\n![](generate/scratch/17592186179719/plots/dd51459cf789b0af1ea77987eaff16d8e00e60580e8b6c254574fce06984e058.png)",
